No good reason, I guess. I'd looked at quite a few of the official=20
translations by that point, and was mostly aiming to convey a similar=20
sentiment, rather than the exact English meaning. Incidentally, some of the=
=20
official translations of that line are quite bizarre; in French, she sings,=
=20
"Le froid est pour moi le prix de la libert=C3=A9" (roughly, "The cold is t=
he=20
price of my freedom") =E2=80=94 almost the exact opposite sentiment!

Your {fanza}-based version works really well though (perhaps with an added=
=20
'cu'). It would break the rhyme pattern in my version, and doesn't have the=
=20
end-of-line alliteration that I liked about mine, but something similar=20
could probably work.

mu'o mi'e .djeikon.

